880,\"output\":420,\"cache_read\":6100,\"cache_write\":1100,\"thought\":180},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"The part I would push back on is the weekly digest in the ticket. It sounds small, but a digest spanning multiple closings needs its own composition step and a different failure story from a single export. I would ship single-closing schedules first and treat the digest as its own piece of work.\\n\\nI've written this up as a plan with four nodes — the delivery log is a blocking dependency for the digest node, so it cannot be quietly skipped.\"},{\"kind\":\"usage\",\"input\":11400,\"output\":236,\"cache_read\":9700,\"cache_write\":180}]},{\"id\":\"review-pull-request\",\"triggers\":[\"review\",\"pull request\",\"pr \",\"diff\",\"look at the changes\"],\"steps\":[{\"kind\":\"thinking\",\"text\":\"The PR reworks contingency deadlines on the escrow timeline, and three surfaces render them. If one closing's extension can shift the calculation, every surface lies at once. Let me read the diff before saying anything.\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"Reading the diff on **#412 — Surface contingency deadlines on the escrow timeline**.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"args\":{\"path\":\"lib/escrow/timeline.dart\"},\"result\":\"class EscrowTimeline {\\n  /// All deadline math goes through the clock so the timeline, the export\\n  /// and the tests share one definition of a business day.\\n  const EscrowTimeline(this._clock);\\n\\n  final EscrowClock _clock;\\n\\n  DateTime deadlineFor(Contingency c, DateTime acceptance) =>\\n      _clock.bd(acceptance, _win(c));\\n\\n  int _win(Contingency c) => switch (c.kind) {\\n        ContingencyKind.inspection => 10,\\n        ContingencyKind.financing => 21,\\n        ContingencyKind.title => 14,\\n      };\\n\\n  int daysRemaining(Contingency c, DateTime acceptance) =>\\n      _clock.bdBetween(_clock.now(), deadlineFor(c, acceptance));\\n}\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"The arithmetic itself is clean — one window table, one clock, deadlines derived instead of typed in. What I want to check is whether two closings can race it: `deadlineFor` resolves from the acceptance date alone, with no closing id in the key.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"args\":{\"pattern\":\"deadlineFor\",\"path\":\"lib/\"},\"result\":\"lib/escrow/timeline.dart:9:  DateTime deadlineFor(Contingency c, DateTime acceptance) =>\\nlib/escrow/timeline_view.dart:44:    final dl = _timeline.deadlineFor(c, closing.acceptedAt);\\nlib/escrow/extensions.dart:71:    final shifted = _timeline.deadlineFor(c, ext.acceptedAt);\\nlib/reports/closing_export.dart:132:    row('Deadline', _timeline.deadlineFor(c, closing.acceptedAt));\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"Three independent callers, and `extensions.dart` recomputes after an extension is recorded. Two closings can share one acceptance date — a re-trade re-accepted the same day, or a double escrow on adjacent parcels — so one closing's extension shifts the other file's inspection, financing and title deadlines everywhere they render. That is the \\\"deadlines moved on their own\\\" report from last month.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"usage\",\"input\":14820,\"output\":386,\"cache_read\":9600,\"cache_write\":1280,\"thought\":210},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"I left one blocking comment on `timeline.dart:9` asking for the closing id in the calculation key, and approved the rest. Days-remaining reads through the clock and needs no migration — that part is correct.\\n\\n**Summary:** 1 blocking comment, 3 nits, no test coverage for the shared-acceptance case.\"},{\"kind\":\"usage\",\"input\":16100,\"output\":194,\"cache_read\":14200,\"cache_write\":240}]},{\"id\":\"triage-ticket\",\"triggers\":[\"triage\",\"ticket\",\"bug report\",\"issue\",\"customer\",\"investigate\"],\"steps\":[{\"kind\":\"thinking\",\"text\":\"Two reports that look alike are not necessarily the same bug. Check whether they share a code path before merging them.\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"Looking at **PD-118 — Inspection PDFs hang at 99% on large scans**. Two brokerages, both on scans over 1GB.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"grep\",\"args\":{\"pattern\":\"onUploadProgress|contentLength\",\"path\":\"lib/documents/\"},\"result\":\"lib/documents/upload_client.dart:52:      onUploadProgress: (sent, total) => _progress.add(sent / total),\\nlib/documents/upload_client.dart:78:    final contentLength = await file.length();\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"read\",\"args\":{\"path\":\"lib/documents/upload_client.dart\",\"offset\":44,\"limit\":22},\"result\":\"  Future<void> upload(File file) async {\\n    final total = await file.length();\\n    await _dio.put(\\n      url,\\n      data: file.openRead(),\\n      onUploadProgress: (sent, _) => _progress.add(sent / total),\\n    );\\n    await _finalize();   // server-side notarization check, ~30s on a 1GB scan\\n    _progress.add(1.0);\\n  }\"},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"It isn't hanging. `onUploadProgress` reaches 100% when the last byte leaves the client, but `_finalize()` then waits on a server-side notarization check that takes ~30s on a 1GB scan. The bar sits at 99% (rounding) with no indication anything is still happening, so the agent assumes it hung and cancels.\\n\\nSo it's a **reporting** bug, not a transfer bug — which also explains why it only shows up on large scans and why the packets that \\\"failed\\\" are actually notarized and present in the closing file.\\n\\n\"},{\"kind\":\"usage\",\"input\":12600,\"output\":358,\"cache_read\":8400,\"cache_write\":1020,\"thought\":140},{\"kind\":\"say\",\"text\":\"I've moved PD-118 to **in progress**, retitled it to name the real cause, and linked PD-121 as a duplicate. The fix is a distinct notarization-pending state rather than a progress percentage — Priya already has it open in **#401**, so I pointed the ticket there instead of starting a second PR.\"},{\"kind\":\"usage\",\"input\":13900,\"output\":188,\"cache_read\":12100,\"cache_write\":210}]}